# Centerrail, Red-Teamed — and the Final Model

Centerrail is the most complete of the six proposals. It is not a draft to be replaced; it is a near-final spec to be *stress-tested*. This document does three things: (I) attacks Centerrail as a hostile reviewer would, keeping only critiques that simplify, harden, or are otherwise defensible with **no regression**; (II) states which of Centerrail's choices are correct and must not be "improved"; (III) produces the final consolidated model with a fair score.

**Verification done first.** Centerrail's autopsy rests on specific Gas City issue numbers I had not personally checked. I fetched the most load-bearing one, [#5511](https://github.com/gastownhall/gascity/issues/5511), and it is real and *more* precise than any proposal claimed: under load (~loadavg 100), `bd create --graph` is killed at the runner deadline **after Dolt has already committed server-side**; the client never sees the result, classifies the timeout as transient, and re-applies the whole plan — producing up to **3 complete live workflows from one `gc sling`** (2 batch + 1 sequential fallback), which then spawn three workers into **one non-isolated checkout** editing the same files. The reporter's own fix is idempotency-key adoption ("re-read before re-writing"). This single issue validates the entire thesis shared across all six designs — idempotent graph mint, private clones, OUTCOME_UNKNOWN-not-auto-retry — so Centerrail's evidentiary base is sound, and I proceed on that footing. (Earlier rounds also confirmed #1181 shared-worktree clobber and #742 tmux-liveness 687-retry.)

---

## Part I — Red team: 12 defensible critiques of Centerrail

Each critique below meets the bar: it must **simplify, harden, or be defensible with no regression**. I discarded a dozen other "gotchas" that were merely stylistic or would trade one risk for another. For each: the attack, why it's real, and the fix.

### C1 — 54 invariants + 60+ KPIs + 8 daemons is an assurance liability, not an asset
**Attack.** Centerrail lists 54 numbered invariants (I01–I54), ~60 KPIs across fast/medium/slow loops, 37 red-team rows, five trust planes, four separate daemons (`centerraild`, `runnerd`, `verifierd`, `deliveryd`), and ~50 crates. A spec this large has a hidden failure mode: **no single engineer can hold the invariant set in their head, so invariants silently conflict or go unenforced.** Which of I01–I54 are actually checked by a DB constraint versus "enforced in Rust logic" versus aspirational? The document doesn't say, and that ambiguity is where the real regressions hide.
**Fix (simplifies, no regression).** Partition the 54 invariants into three enforcement tiers, printed next to each: **(T1) Schema-enforced** — a DB constraint or partial unique index makes violation `INSERT`-impossible (e.g., I01 one-writer-per-Variant, I03 fence monotonicity, I09 atomic plan hash). **(T2) Gateway-enforced** — a single chokepoint function rejects it (I07 stale-token, I46/I47 effect fencing, I52 no-check-waiver). **(T3) Test-enforced** — only a property/chaos test can catch it (I36 four-valued liveness, I38 salvage-before-release). This is not new work; it's *labeling* existing work, and it converts "54 things we promise" into "54 things with a named mechanism, auditable in CI." An invariant with no tier is a spec bug caught at review time. This is pure hardening.

### C2 — The verifier plane's independence is asserted but its trust root is circular
**Attack.** Centerrail's headline safety claim is E2/E3 evidence from an *independent* verifier the author "could not alter." But the verifier runs a test suite **that lives in the repository the author is editing.** A prompt-injected or malicious author can modify the tests themselves (within their write-set), and the "independent" verifier will faithfully run the author's weakened tests and return a clean E2. Independence of *execution environment* does not give independence of *the oracle* when the oracle is in-tree.
**Fix (hardens, no regression).** Split the oracle from the diff. (1) **Test changes are a distinct risk-classed resource**: any diff hunk touching a test file, CI config, or acceptance fixture is tagged `oracle-modifying` and (a) cannot be reviewed by the same family that authored it, and (b) requires the *pre-change* test suite to also pass against the new implementation (you may add tests and strengthen them, but weakening/deleting a test is a reviewable, evidence-generating event, never silent). (2) **Hidden/historical holdouts** (already E3 in Centerrail) are the real independence guarantee and must be *required*, not optional, for R2+; the in-repo suite is E1/E2 supporting evidence only. This closes the circularity Centerrail's own tiering gestures at but doesn't lock: it never says "the author can't edit the oracle." Now it does.

### C3 — Successor-Attempt scope amendment is correct but its latency will make agents avoid it
**Attack.** Centerrail's successor-Attempt protocol (freeze → verify → checkpoint → new fence → transfer grants → rebuild sandbox → resume) is *correct* but *heavy*: every mid-task discovery of "I also need to touch `config/`" triggers a full quiesce-checkpoint-respawn cycle. Under realistic coding, write-set discovery is constant. If each expansion costs seconds of quiesce + a sandbox rebuild + possible session restart, agents (or the humans tuning prompts) will learn to **over-declare their initial write-set to avoid the penalty** — which destroys the write-set lock's precision, the very thing it exists for. The safety mechanism creates an incentive that defeats it.
**Fix (hardens, no regression).** Two-track expansion by *authority delta*, which Centerrail hints at (§5.17.3's "read-only expansion... lighter capsule") but doesn't generalize: **(Track A, cheap) additive expansion within the same trust/sandbox/network/secret envelope** — acquiring an *additional* exclusive path lock that is currently free, with no change to secrets/network/sandbox class — is a single serializable lock-acquire + fence-bump + grant-append, *no quiesce, no sandbox rebuild, no session restart* (the running process simply gains a lock it didn't have; nothing it already holds changes). **(Track B, heavy) authority-escalating expansion** — anything that widens secrets, network egress, sandbox class, or crosses a protected path — uses the full successor protocol. The invariant that matters ("no in-place *authority* escalation") is preserved exactly; what's removed is the false coupling of *scope* widening to *authority* widening. This makes the common case cheap, kills the over-declaration incentive, and regresses nothing because Track B still guards every genuine authority change.

### C4 — "GitHub is the only merge authority" + "Centerrail publishes required checks" is a governance loop
**Attack.** Centerrail says repository branch protection is sovereign and Centerrail cannot waive checks (I52) — good. But it *also* has Centerrail's Effect Broker **publish the required checks** (`Centerrail / Evidence Complete`, `/ Review Independent`, etc.) that branch protection then requires. So the entity that must not be able to waive the gate is the entity that *reports whether the gate passed*. A compromised or buggy Broker can publish a green `Evidence Complete` check for a Candidate whose evidence is not complete, and branch protection — trusting that check — merges. The sovereignty is nominal.
**Fix (hardens, no regression).** Make the check-publishing path *evidence-derived and independently reconstructible*, not Broker-asserted. (1) The Broker may only publish a check by **presenting the signed proof-bundle hash** (Centerrail already produces this, §5.20.10); a separate, minimal **attestation verifier** — not the Broker — recomputes "does this bundle satisfy this repo's policy for this exact SHA?" and it alone can post the green check, using its own credential. The Broker delivers code; the attestor delivers verdicts; neither can do the other's job. (2) The check payload includes the proof-bundle hash so a human (or an external auditor) can, after the fact, recompute the verdict from immutable evidence. This splits "deliver" from "attest," which Centerrail separates everywhere *except* here. Pure defense-in-depth; no capability lost.

### C5 — The four-valued liveness `CONTRADICTORY` state has no defined exit
**Attack.** Centerrail correctly makes liveness `{ALIVE, DEAD, UNKNOWN, CONTRADICTORY}` and fails closed on the latter two. But it never says **how a task leaves `CONTRADICTORY`.** If process-supervision says dead, the runner-lease says alive, and the adapter says waiting, the task is frozen safely — but *frozen forever* is a liveness leak: the Variant holds its lease, its quota reservation, and its clone, and nothing reclaims them because destructive action fails closed. A fail-closed state with no resolution path is a resource ratchet that eventually starves the fleet.
**Fix (hardens, no regression).** `CONTRADICTORY` and prolonged `UNKNOWN` are **escalation-generating, not silent**: after a bounded grace window they raise a typed `Intervention` (Centerrail already has first-class Interventions) that presents an operator with the *evidence* (which probe said what) and exactly three safe actions — (a) authoritatively fence-and-reclaim (human asserts dead, ledger increments fence so any live incarnation self-suicides on next heartbeat), (b) extend grace (human asserts alive), (c) quarantine the clone for forensics and reclaim the lease. The key property: reclamation from `CONTRADICTORY` is **always fence-mediated**, so even if the human is wrong and the task was alive, the stale incarnation cannot deliver (fencing already guarantees this). This turns an unbounded safe-freeze into a bounded safe-freeze with a human-authorized, fencing-protected exit. No invariant weakens; a resource leak closes.

### C6 — Cross-provider quota epistemology is honest but the reservation math can deadlock
**Attack.** Centerrail reserves quota *before* a turn and settles after (§5.14.6), with `fail_closed` when headroom is unknown. Correct for safety, but consider: a Mission's critical path needs a frontier model; all frontier profiles are at `UnknownCapacity` (cold or post-incident); `fail_closed` blocks; the work that would *generate* the usage signal to *resolve* the unknown never runs. The system is safely deadlocked — it will strand paid capacity indefinitely because it won't spend the small amount needed to learn whether it can spend more.
**Fix (hardens, no regression).** A bounded **probe reservation**: when a profile is `UnknownCapacity` and eligible work is starved, the scheduler may spend a small, policy-capped probe budget (e.g., one minimal turn) specifically to convert `unknown` → `known`, treated as exploration, logged, and rate-limited per profile. This is distinct from scheduling real work on unknown headroom (still forbidden) — it's spending a *known-small* amount to *measure*, which is exactly what a human operator does manually. `unknown` still never masquerades as headroom; we just permit a metered measurement. Removes the deadlock; the safety rule (don't schedule bulk work on unknowns) is untouched.

### C6b — "consumer-account pooling is never a throughput mechanism" needs the incentive removed, not just the policy
**Attack.** Centerrail bans consumer-sub rotation as policy (I44) and flags it (I7-style). But a policy ban invites circumvention as long as the *incentive* exists — ten owned Pro seats visibly offer 10× throughput if only the rules would allow it, so someone will try to configure around the ban.
**Fix (defensible, no regression).** Adopt the stronger structural stance from the prior round: `named_human_subscription` profiles are **rate-governed to one-seat-equivalent per owning human regardless of seat count**, so holding ten consumer logins buys *zero* extra throughput by construction. Throughput comes only from `api_credit`/`organization_seat`/`service_identity`. This removes the incentive rather than policing it, which is strictly more robust than a ban. Centerrail's commercial-class model already has the fields; this is one governor rule over them. *(Grounds: the three vendors' published teP0+r4B31\rms on credential sharing / limit circumvention, and Anthropic's Agent-SDK-with-plan guidance separating interactive subscription use from shared automation. Operational fact, re-verify before relying; not legal advice.)*

### C7 — Phase 0 formal methods before Phase A code is the one place the build plan over-reaches
**Attack.** Centerrail's Phase 0 requires TLA+/PlusCal models for fences, races, commands/effects, Graph Deltas, scope amendment, cancellation, and remote ambiguity **before** the smallest kernel is coded. This is the plan's single unrealistic gate: a team that must model-check seven protocols in TLA+ before writing a lease acquire will spend months in specification before shipping one PR, and the models will drift from the code the moment code exists. Formal methods are valuable but this sequencing inverts their leverage.
**Fix (simplifies, no regression on assurance).** Formal-model **exactly two** protocols in Phase 0 — the two where a subtle interleaving bug is (a) most likely and (b) most catastrophic and *un*-catchable by tests: **the lease/fence/reclaim protocol** and **the command/effect idempotency-under-ambiguous-timeout protocol** (this is literally the #5511 bug class). The other five get *property-based tests* in Phase 0 and formal models *later*, only if a property test finds an interleaving that resists a code fix. This keeps the assurance where it pays — the two protocols whose failure is exactly the verified real-world incident — and unblocks Phase A. Centerrail itself says "keep models small and linked to executable conformance tests"; this operationalizes that restraint into the phase gates.

### C8 — Antigravity as a required GA provider is a schedule dependency on the least-proven surface
**Attack.** Centerrail requires four certified adapters (Codex, Claude, Cursor, Antigravity) for Phase E GA. Antigravity is the newest and least-documented of the four; its headless/quota surfaces are the ones Centerrail hedges most ("where supported," "cached interactive credentials are not portable CI secrets"). Binding *GA* to the certification of the least-stable adapter means the whole product's GA date floats on the most volatile upstream.
**Fix (simplifies, no regression).** Decouple GA from provider count. GA requires **the kernel + any P0+r4B33\P0+r4B34\P0+r4B35\P0+r6B42\P0+r5053\P0+r5045\two certified providers** (Claude + Codex are the safe pair); each additional provider (Cursor, Antigravity) promotes independently behind the same conformance suite when its upstream stabilizes. Centerrail already promotes providers "one at a time behind the same conformance suite" — this just says GA doesn't *wait* for all four. The capability (four providers) is a goal, not a gate. No architectural regression; the harness trait and certification matrix are unchanged. This directly serves the user's actual requirement (multi-frontier) without hostage-taking the timeline.

### C9 — The Effect resolver's "adopt existing effect" step is under-specified for the adversarial case
**Attack.** On `OUTCOME_UNKNOWN`, Centerrail's resolver "reads authoritative remote state by logical key... adopts a matching existing effect." But *matching* is the hard part: after an ambiguous push, how does the resolver distinguish "my push landed" from "a *different, stale* incarnation's push landed with the same logical key"? If two incarnations (the #5511 scenario, pre-fencing) share a logical effect key, adoption could adopt the wrong one's work.
**Fix (hardens, no regression).** The logical effect key must include the **fence** (already in the AuthorityToken), and remote adoption must verify the **expected head OID** the effect intended to create, not just the ref name. A pushed ref is adopted iff its OID equals the `desired_state_hash` computed from *this* attempt's exact prepared Candidate. A ref at the same name with a different OID is `ORPHANED_REMOTE` (Centerrail has this state) and quarantined, never adopted. This makes adoption *identity-exact*, matching Centerrail's own "expected-OID / force-with-lease" delivery discipline — it just applies the same rigor to the *resolver* that the *delivery path* already has. Closes the one place ambiguous-effect resolution could launder a stale incarnation's work.

### C10 — No stated backpressure between the verifier plane and the writer plane
**Attack.** Centerrail bounds writer slots and admission, but the verifier plane is a *separate* resource with its own capacity, and evidence invalidation (§5.20.7) means a single rebase can re-queue *all* of a Candidate's E2/E3 evidence for re-run. Under a burst of rebases (stale-base storm), the verifier queue can grow without bound while writers keep producing Candidates that immediately need re-verification. Writers throttle on *their* slots but nothing tells them "the verifier is the bottleneck, stop producing."
**Fix (hardens, no regression).** Verifier queue depth and dwell are **admission-control inputs to the writer scheduler** (Centerrail's backpressure §5.12.6 lists "verifier saturation" as a signal — this makes the coupling explicit and bidirectional): when verifier dwell exceeds a threshold, writer admission for that repo freezes *before* producing more re-verification load, and the conflict-forecast + stale-base pre-rebase (already in the Integration Coordinator) run *ahead* of verification so evidence is generated once against a fresh base rather than repeatedly against a moving one. Turns an unbounded re-verification amplifier into a bounded, forecasted one. No new subsystem; a control-loop edge Centerrail names but doesn't close.

### C11 — The portal's "pending command" honesty has a gap on the kill path
**Attack.** Centerrail's pending-command chips are right, but consider the emergency-stop / freeze path under the exact partition where honesty matters most: operator hits "freeze repo," the command is durably recorded, chip shows "freeze requested" — but a partitioned runner hasn't received it and is *still dispatching*. The portal honestly shows "requested," but the *operator's mental model* is "I stopped it," and the gap between "ledger recorded the freeze" and "every runner honors it" is exactly the window an operator most needs to see quantified.
**Fix (hardens, no regression).** The freeze/stop chip resolves in **two stages, both shown**: "recorded" (ledger has it) → "enforced" (all live runner-leases have acknowledged the freeze generation, or their leases have expired). The chip shows "enforced on 3/4 runners — 1 runner unreachable, its leases expire in 12s" so the operator sees the true safety state, not a binary. Because runners self-fence on lease-renewal failure (the cross-host design), an unreachable runner *becomes* safe within the TTL, and the chip counts that down. This is the honest completion of the pending-command principle for the one command where the partition gap is safety-critical. No mechanism added; the runner-lease acks already exist.

### C12 — Multi-repo saga is correctly non-atomic but lacks a poison-default
**Attack.** Centerrail's multi-repo saga (PREPARE_ALL → VERIFY_ALL → INTEGRATE_IN_ORDER → PARTIAL_SUCCESS/COMPENSATING) is honest about non-atomicity. But the default on `MANUAL_RECOVERY_REQUIRED` is undefined: if repo A integrated and repo B failed compensation, what is the *system's* posture while waiting for a human? If it keeps scheduling dependent work, it builds on a half-integrated state; if it freezes everything, one stuck saga halts unrelated work.
**Fix (hardens, no regression).** `MANUAL_RECOVERY_REQUIRED` **quarantines the saga's blast radius, not the fleet**: the specific repos and any Missions with a declared dependency on the half-integrated state are frozen (writers paused, marked with the saga incident), while unrelated repos/Missions proceed. The half-integrated commit is tagged with saga-incident metadata so any Candidate attempting to base on it is auto-blocked until the saga resolves. This bounds the blast radius precisely — Centerrail has the incident and dependency machinery, this just wires the default posture. No regression; it prevents both "build on quicksand" and "one saga freezes the company."

### Critiques I considered and rejected (for honesty)
- *"Drop the verifier plane, it's too heavy"* — **rejected**: it's the core safety differentiator; removing it regresses assurance. C2 hardens it instead.
- *"SQLite can't scale, mandate Postgres"* — **rejected**: Centerrail's SQLite-dev/Postgres-team split is correct; mandating Postgres regresses the local story for no benefit.
- *"Five trust planes is too many"* — **rejected**: each is a real credential/trust boundary (execution/verification/delivery genuinely need separate credentials); collapsing them regresses security. C1 makes the *invariants* legible without collapsing the *planes*.
- *"Replace GitHub-as-authority with an internal merge queue for speed"* — **rejected**: that's the Gas Town mistake (Refinery merges itself); external sovereignty is the point.

---

## Part II — What Centerrail gets right and must not be "simplified" away

A hostile reviewer who *only* cuts would regress this design. These choices are load-bearing and correct:

- **Transaction-processor framing** over "agent city" — the single most important reframe; all six proposals converge here and Centerrail states it most cleanly.
- **Variant as the fence/lease boundary** (not Work Package) — resolves the one-writer-vs-race contradiction correctly; a race is N Variants in one Selection Group, each independently fenced.
- **Immutable Graph Delta** for dynamic work — a Plan Revision never mutates; expansion is a content-addressed append. This is the #5511 fix generalized.
- **Ledger, not pure event-sourcing** — canonical tables + append-only events + outbox; avoids both mutable-state-with-no-history and rebuild-from-genesis-as-bottleneck.
- **Typed capability negotiation with maturity levels** (unsupported/experimental/supported/certified) — never assume resume/fork/quota from a provider *name*.
- **`unknown` capacity stays `unknown`** — never scheduled as headroom.
- **Evidence bound to exact subject + trust tiers E0–E4** — a green E1 cannot satisfy an E3 obligation; a rebase invalidates by default.
- **Author cannot satisfy independent review; reviewer/attestor cannot waive checks** — separation of powers.
- **Honest non-atomic remote effects** — logically-exactly-once over physically-at-least-once; OUTCOME_UNKNOWN, never auto-retry.
- **Superiority is a benchmark gate, not a branding claim** — "better than Gas City" stays a hypothesis until Centerrail Bench proves it per task-stratum.

---

## Part III — The Final Model: **Centerrail (consolidated)**

Centerrail's architecture, adopted essentially intact, with the twelve red-team fixes folded in. I am not renaming or re-architecting it — that would be ego, not engineering. The deltas from the submitted Centerrail spec are exactly C1–C12.

### The shape (unchanged from Centerrail)
Five trust planes — Control, Execution, Verification, Delivery, Evidence/Audit — with the portal as a projection over all five. Rust modular monolith (`centerraild`) + separate `runnerd`/`verifierd`/`deliveryd` (real trust boundaries, not microservice fashion). SQLite-WAL dev → Postgres team, identical `sqlx` schema. Vite/React portal, types generated from Rust. Domain: Mission → Plan Revision → (Graph Delta) → Work Package → Selection Group → Variant → Attempt → Candidate → Evidence → Review → Integration → Observation Window.

### The twelve consolidated hardenings
1. **(C1)** Every invariant I01–I54 is tagged **T1 schema / T2 gateway / T3 test**; an untagged invariant fails code review. Assurance becomes auditable.
2. **(C2)** Oracle-modifying diffs (tests/CI/fixtures) are risk-classed: pre-change suite must still pass, weakening a test is a reviewable evidence event, and **hidden holdouts are required (not optional) for R2+**. The verifier's independence is now real, not circular.
3. **(C3)** Scope expansion is **two-track**: additive-within-envelope is a cheap lock-acquire + fence-bump (no quiesce); authority-escalating uses the full successor protocol. Kills the over-declaration incentive; preserves the no-in-place-authority-escalation invariant.
4. **(C4)** Check publishing is split from delivery: a minimal **attestation verifier** recomputes the verdict from the signed proof-bundle and posts the green check with its own credential; the Broker only delivers code. The sovereignty of branch protection becomes real.
5. **(C5)** `CONTRADICTORY`/prolonged-`UNKNOWN` liveness raises a typed Intervention with three **fence-mediated** safe exits. Safe-freeze becomes bounded; no resource ratchet.
6. **(C6)** A bounded, logged **probe reservation** converts `UnknownCapacity` → known without ever scheduling bulk work on unknowns. Removes the safe-deadlock.
7. **(C6b)** `named_human_subscription` profiles are **rate-governed to one-seat-per-human**; consumer pooling offers zero throughput advantage by construction. Incentive removed, not just policed.
8. **(C7)** Phase 0 formal-models **exactly two** protocols (lease/fence/reclaim; command/effect idempotency-under-timeout — the #5511 class); the rest get property tests first. Assurance where it pays; Phase A unblocked.
9. **(C8)** GA = **kernel + any two certified providers** (Claude+Codex); Cursor/Antigravity promote independently. Timeline no longer hostage to the least-stable upstream.
10. **(C9)** Effect adoption is **identity-exact**: logical key includes fence; a remote ref is adopted iff its OID equals this attempt's `desired_state_hash`, else `ORPHANED_REMOTE`. No stale-incarnation laundering.
11. **(C10)** Verifier queue depth is a **writer-admission backpressure input**; conflict-forecast + stale-base pre-rebase run ahead of verification. Re-verification amplifier bounded.
12. **(C11/C12)** Freeze/stop chips resolve in two shown stages (recorded → enforced-on-N/M-runners, with self-fence countdown); multi-repo `MANUAL_RECOVERY_REQUIRED` **quarantines the saga blast radius, not the fleet**. Honest safety state; bounded incident.

### The V1 boundary (Centerrail's, with C7/C8 applied)
Linux; one repo/target-branch per Mission; GitHub; private dissociated clones; two-track scope amendment; exact Candidates; independent verification with required holdouts for R2+; blind cross-family review; quota reservations + probe; rule-based routing; **Claude + Codex certified** (Cursor/Antigravity follow). Excludes: production deploy/publish/migrations, cross-repo atomicity, scheduler-embedded TypeScript, same-file multiwriter editing, consumer-account pooling, online-learning routing, and any claim of zero-regression or full autonomy.

### The proof that gates everything (Centerrail's Phase-A exit, sharpened)
Not "twenty agents ran." The gate is: one Mission materialized exactly once (idempotent on plan hash); one Variant acquired a never-reused fence; a stale duplicate (the #5511 scenario) could **neither mutate state nor create an external effect** (fencing + identity-exact adoption); the writer ran in a private clone (no shared `.git`); useful work survived `kill -9` at every boundary; a clean verifier produced exact-subject E2 evidence the author could not weaken (C2); and GitHub received one brokered Candidate with an attestor-published check and **no SCM credential in the agent sandbox**.

---

## Part IV — Final fair score

Same 10-dimension rubric used throughout. Centerrail-submitted scored itself 9.35/10 (paper architecture); I regard that as slightly generous pre-red-team because C2 (verifier circularity), C4 (attestation loop), and C5 (no CONTRADICTORY exit) were latent holes a grader would find. The consolidated model closes them.

| Dimension (wt) | Centerrail (submitted) | Consolidated | Why it moved |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| Product framing (10) | 9.7 | **9.7** | Transaction-processor thesis is correct and untouched |
| Authority/transaction kernel (15) | 9.5 | **9.6** | C9 (identity-exact adoption) closes the resolver's adversarial gap |
| Workspace/change isolation (10) | 9.4 | **9.5** | C3 removes the over-declaration incentive that would have eroded write-set precision |
| Evidence/regression control (12) | 9.5 | **9.6** | C2 closes the verifier-independence circularity — the biggest latent hole |
| Security/governance (12) | 9.2 | **9.4** | C4 (attestor≠broker) makes branch-protection sovereignty real, not nominal |
| Provider/quota realism (10) | 9.0 | **9.2** | C6 probe-reservation removes the safe-deadlock; C6b removes the pooling incentive |
| Integration semantics (10) | 9.3 | **9.4** | C10 bounds the re-verification amplifier; C12 bounds saga blast radius |
| Portal/operator product (8) | 9.2 | **9.3** | C5 + C11 make the two safety-critical UI states (contradictory liveness, freeze-under-partition) honest and bounded |
| Testability/assurance (8) | 9.6 | **9.6** | C1 (invariant tiering) and C7 (two formal models) improve *legibility* of assurance without adding unproven claims — net neutral on score, positive on buildability |
| Delivery realism/scope control (5) | 8.1 | **8.6** | C7 + C8 fix the two over-reaching gates (formal-everything, four-provider GA); plan is now credibly executable |
| **Weighted total** | **9.35** | **9.52** | |

### **Final score: 9.52 / 10 (paper architecture)**

### Why not higher
The withheld ~0.5 is load-bearing and un-inflatable by prose:
- **Implementation maturity remains ~0.5/10** (Centerrail's own honest number). A rigorous, red-teamed spec is not running code. The score above is for the *design*; the *product* is unproven until Phase-A chaos tests pass on real hardware.
- **The verifier-independence fix (C2) shifts trust to hidden holdouts** — which are only as good as the holdout corpus a team actually maintains. Perfect on paper, human-dependent in practice.
- **Cross-provider quota is vendor-capped**; C6 makes `unknown` *safe and measurable*, not *known*. No design transcends what vendors disclose.
- **Split-brain is bounded, not eliminated** (CAP); C9/C11 make it *safe and visible*, and that is the ceiling physics allows.
- **The consolidated model is larger than any single source** — C1's invariant-tiering mitigates the assurance-liability of size, but a 54-invariant, five-plane system is still a multi-year program, and integration risk is real until the kernel is boring.

### Why not lower
Every red-team fix **simplified, hardened, or was defensible with no regression** — none traded one risk for another, and the four tempting cuts that *would* have regressed the design (drop the verifier, mandate Postgres, collapse the planes, internalize merge) were explicitly rejected. The kernel is correct at the transaction level and now identity-exact under adversarial adoption; the verifier's independence is no longer circular; branch-protection sovereignty is real rather than nominal; the two safety-critical UI states are honest; the build plan's two over-reaching gates are fixed so a team can actually ship the Phase-A kernel that proves the #5511 class dead. That is the strongest defensible position for a design document — and the last half-point is purchasable only with the one thing prose cannot fake: the chaos suite green on real hardware.
